read-string-does-not-skip-comments — (read "string") ignores load's comment-stripping on real BricsCAD, unlike clautolisp
============================================================================================================================

Status: OPEN. Found via a real, reproducible parity gap while building
an unrelated project (autocad-gui-prototype, loader.lsp), 2026-09-08.

Summary
-------

  (read ";; foo\nbar")

  BricsCAD V26 (real, macOS)  -> NIL
  clautolisp                 -> BAR

`load` (reading a form from a FILE) skips `;`-comments before handing
a form to the reader, on both sides -- that much is consistent. But
`read` applied directly to a STRING containing leading comment lines
does NOT skip them on real BricsCAD: it returns NIL (as if EOF were
reached immediately), while clautolisp's own `read` strips comments
the same way `load` does, silently matching a naive assumption about
AutoLISP source-file handling rather than (apparently) real AutoLISP
reader semantics on a bare string.

Concretely (autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp): a small loader read a
metadata file (a foo.alpm, itself starting with four `;;;;` header-
comment lines, then a real `(alpm-define-system ...)` form) via
read-line + strcat (reconstructing the whole file into one string) and
a single `(read whole-string)` on the result. Under clautolisp this
returned the expected form every time headless -- 130/130 tests green,
including an end-to-end reload of loader.lsp. On real BricsCAD V26 it
silently returned NIL, every single time, for all three files
affected (each starting with the same four-line comment header) --
plain `load`-based file loads elsewhere in the exact same project were
completely unaffected (they never call `read` on a string, only
`load` on a file). Confirmed via a live BricsCAD probe: the
reconstructed string was verified byte-correct (exact length, exact
content, comments included) via `strlen`/`princ`, then `(read that-
string)` still came back NIL. Root cause pinned down only after
several other hypotheses (SECURELOAD/TRUSTEDPATHS, boundp reliability
on `C:` symbols, literal-path identity, `foreach` vs. plain function
nesting, file encoding) were each tested live on BricsCAD and ruled
out first — this is the confirmed, reproducible bottom of it, not a
guess.

To check
--------

  1. The autolisp-spec pages for READ — does the spec text say
     anything about comment-skipping being a LOAD/file-reader
     responsibility rather than READ's own? If the spec is silent or
     wrong here, it needs a correction either way.
  2. Real AutoCAD (not just BricsCAD) — same probe
     `(read ";; foo\nbar")`: does AutoCAD's reader behave like
     BricsCAD's (NIL) or like clautolisp's (BAR)? If AutoCAD and
     BricsCAD disagree with each other too, this may be a genuine
     BricsCAD-specific quirk rather than a general AutoLISP-family
     read semantics question — changes what "correct" means here.
  3. Whether this is really "no comment support in read at all" or
     something narrower — e.g. maybe a comment is tolerated mid-string
     (between two real tokens) but not as the very FIRST thing read
     sees; the probe above only exercises the "string starts with
     comments" case, not comments in other positions. Worth a small
     matrix before deciding the fix (none attempted here — this issue
     is the found-and-triaged report, not the full characterization).

Fix
---

Once (1)/(2)/(3) are answered:

  - If real AutoLISP (either vendor) genuinely never skips comments in
    `read`-on-a-string, clautolisp's own `read` is the one that's
    wrong (too lenient) and should stop silently accepting a comment-
    led string — at minimum matching the stricter vendor behavior
    under --autocad/--bricscad dialects, possibly staying lenient
    under --clautolisp (own dialect) if that leniency is worth keeping
    as a deliberate, PER-DIALECT convenience.
  - Either way, this looks like exactly the kind of gap
    deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue's mechanism
    should eventually flag — not "operator doesn't exist in this
    dialect" (READ exists everywhere) but "operator behaves
    differently across dialects, worth a warning when a caller's
    result would differ from a stricter/vendor dialect's". If that
    general warning mechanism isn't close to landing, a narrower,
    read-specific warning (or at minimum a documented BricsCAD/AutoCAD
    caveat on READ's own spec page) is worth doing on its own,
    smaller track — a headless CI run in the downstream project would
    have caught this immediately instead of burning most of a day of
    live-BricsCAD trial and error to isolate it.

Workaround shipped downstream (autocad-gui-prototype, not clautolisp
itself): the caller now strips comment-only lines itself before ever
calling `read`, rather than relying on `read` to do it — see
loader.lsp `fs-loader--comment-line-p` / `fs-loader--slurp`,
commit bfc057d.

Found by
--------

autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp live-BricsCAD debugging session,
2026-09-08 (Claude Sonnet 5, with pjb driving BricsCAD interactively).
Related: deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue (the
general per-dialect-behavior warning mechanism this could plug into).
